{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c5ab","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a070b0-22c9-7f91-ad93-24f3d289098d"},"sequence":304,"recorded_at":1780531400000603,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"f9c11a89-ee4c-4cfe-aa7e-08a834791d50","payload_type":"tool.result","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"tool_result","command_id":"f9c11a89-ee4c-4cfe-aa7e-08a834791d50","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"f9c11a89-ee4c-4cfe-aa7e-08a834791d50"},"call_id":"call_01a070b29b7a7933aa5025b825ea702a","text":"{\n  \"chunk_id\": \"exec-6-1\",\n  \"command\": \"rm internal/terminalbackend/temp_probe_test.go && git status --short\",\n  \"description\": \"Delete scratch probe file\",\n  \"exit_code\": 0,\n  \"terminal_status\": \"completed\",\n  \"output\": \"M  LOGBOOK.md\\nMM README.md\\nA  internal/terminalbackend/conformance.go\\nA  internal/terminalbackend/conformance_test.go\\nMM internal/terminalbackend/internal_pin_test.go\\n M internal/terminalbackend/manifest.go\\nMM internal/terminalbackend/manifest_test.go\\nAM internal/terminalbackend/refusal_arm_inventory_test.go\\n M internal/terminalbackend/terminalbackend.go\\nMM internal/terminalbackend/terminalbackend_test.go\\nMM internal/traceability/cmd/tracecheck/main_test.go\\nMM internal/traceability/ownership.v0.5.0.json\\nMM internal/traceability/traceability.go\\nMM internal/traceability/traceability_test.go\\n\",\n  \"original_output_bytes\": 596,\n  \"original_output_tokens\": 149,\n  \"truncated\": false\n}","correlation_facts":{"tool_name":"bash","outcome":"success"}}}
{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c5ad","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a070b0-22c9-7f91-ad93-24f3d289098d"},"sequence":305,"recorded_at":1780531400000605,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"f9c11a89-ee4c-4cfe-aa7e-08a834791d50","payload_type":"tool.result","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"tool_result","command_id":"f9c11a89-ee4c-4cfe-aa7e-08a834791d50","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"f9c11a89-ee4c-4cfe-aa7e-08a834791d50"},"call_id":"call_01a070b29d3e76a1bb7f5881159fe021","text":"Read text file `internal/terminalbackend/refusal_arm_inventory_test.go`.\n1|// AST-derived refusal-arm inventory for the terminal backend packages.\n2|//\n3|// The failure this file exists to prevent: a brand-new production refusal\n4|// arm carrying a brand-new unasserted detail shipped while `gofmt`,\n5|// `go vet`, `go test ./...`, and `tracecheck` all stayed green. A\n6|// hand-maintained arm list cannot catch that; a derived one can, but only\n7|// if it is checked in BOTH directions. The forward direction alone passes\n8|// vacuously on a truncated derivation: every derived arm asserted is true\n9|// when the derivation silently dropped half the package. So this file\n10|// derives every refusal arm from the production AST, requires each to\n11|// resolve to exactly one declared row naming a real asserting test, and\n12|// requires every declared row to resolve to exactly one derived arm.\n13|//\n14|// Modelled on internal/canonicaljson/grammar_inventory_test.go: derivation\n15|// fails closed on an empty, short, or unparseable input, and the declared\n16|// table cannot move its own goalposts because the derivation never\n17|// consults it.\n18|//\n19|// Stated bounds (not inferred):\n20|//   - The inventory proves every arm is DECLARED with a resolving test.\n21|//     Execution and decision proof is each row's named test, measured by\n22|//     this package's suite. A row resolves textually (detail mention, or\n23|//     entry-plus-code/predicate mention); a wrongly attributed row that\n24|//     still resolves is a review finding, not a green light.\n25|//   - Attribution drift survives through that fallback: a\n26|//     dead-by-construction arm can resolve through a sibling site's test\n27|//     while pointing at the wrong guard. Live example: CheckTransition's\n28|//     \"operation vocabulary\" arm (conformance.go) is unreachable —\n29|//     ParseOperation admits exactly the ten operations transitionTable\n30|//     covers, so lookupTransition never misses — yet the row carries no\n31|//     bound and resolves through TestParseOperationAdmitsOnlyTheTenClosedOperations,\n32|//     which drives ParseOperation's own arm sharing the detail. The five\n33|//     re-parse arms are the contrast: they declare boundDefensiveReparse\n34|//     and TestDefensiveBoundsAreExactlyThese pins the set.\n35|//   - Kill inflation: both inventory directions fail on ANY added or\n36|//     deleted production arm by construction, so an arm-deleting mutant\n37|//     elsewhere reads as killed by the inventory alone with no behavioral\n38|//     change. Mutation scores measured with this file present are NOT\n39|//     comparable to pre-inventory scores; review the failing-test list,\n40|//     not just the exit code.\n41|//   - errors.New/fmt.Errorf sites are not wire refusals and carry no arm.\n42|//     They live only in validatePlatforms (funneled into one *Error arm\n43|//     whose detail set is pinned exactly) and DigestFile (I/O errors that\n44|//     must never be *Error). Both properties are asserted below, in every\n45|//     FuncDecl body plus every package-level var initializer, and any\n46|//     reference to either constructor outside direct-call position\n47|//     (e.g. `newPlain := errors.New`) fails the suite, so an aliased\n48|//     construction cannot ship unattributed.\n49|//   - The DigestFile half of that allowlist is function-wide with no\n50|//     detail pin, unlike validatePlatforms' derived exact set: a new\n51|//     errors.New on any path inside DigestFile passes the gate (and the\n52|//     never-wire behavioral assertion, which only drives the current\n53|//     paths). Disclosed, not closed.\n54|//   - Custom error types outside the two recognised spellings (*Error,\n55|//     errors.New, fmt.Errorf) are outside both derivations: a bespoke\n56|//     error type smuggled as a refusal survives. Disclosed, not closed.\n57|//   - Constructor Details are single static literals by assertion: the\n58|//     derivation fatals on any mismatchf/integrityFailure call without\n59|//     exactly one string-literal argument, so format-verb interpolation\n60|//     cannot enter the table. Zero multi-argument sites today; go vet's\n61|//     printf analyzer independently refuses a non-constant format string.\n62|package terminalbackend\n63|\n64|import (\n65|\t\"errors\"\n66|\t\"go/ast\"\n67|\t\"go/parser\"\n68|\t\"go/token\"\n69|\t\"os\"\n70|\t\"path/filepath\"\n71|\t\"strconv\"\n72|\t\"strings\"\n73|\t\"testing\"\n74|)\n75|\n76|// refusalArm is one derived production refusal: a wire-code *Error\n77|// construction site keyed by file, enclosing function (receiver-qualified\n78|// for methods), code symbol, static detail, and occurrence index within\n79|// that key. The index keeps two identical arms in one function distinct:\n80|// adding a second arm with an already-declared key fails on the missing\n(more lines below)","correlation_facts":{"tool_name":"read_file","outcome":"success"}}}
